# /log-backlog

Append a single new entry to `harness-backlog.md` per the file's documented entry format, and — when `project.yaml` declares a `backlog_upstream` repo — file the same entry to the fleet's shared issue inbox so it reaches the template maintainer without a manual relay. Entry prefixes are **read at runtime, never hardcoded** (see Steps). This skill is the operational ritual for the harness-backlog logging discipline declared in `core/governance/CLAUDE.md` § Session discipline.

## When to use

Use when a backlog-candidate issue is surfaced in conversation and you want to formally capture it. The discipline rule (CLAUDE.md): any issue that is a property of the **harness template** rather than this project's content goes here.

Includes:
- Bugs in init scripts (`init.ps1`, `init.sh`, `init-validate.*`)
- Missing files in the harness default tree
- Naming collisions with project conventions
- Missing capabilities that operators have to install post-init
- Methodology deltas between harness docs and installed-skill protocols
- Schema gaps in `project.yaml.schema.json`
- UX traps in error messages

When unclear whether something is harness-template or project-content, log it. Easier to reclassify than to recover.

## When NOT to use

- Issues that are project-content rather than harness-template (those go in this project's DECISIONS log, kill-criteria, or relevant SOP — not in the harness backlog)
- Inferred issues Claude noticed but the operator hasn't endorsed; this skill captures issues someone is explicitly raising, not background suspicions

## Required inputs

If any required field is missing from the invocation, ask the operator before writing:

- **title** — short noun-phrase title for the entry header (≤ 80 chars)
- **severity** — one of `blocking` / `standard` / `nice-to-have`
- **where** — file paths in the harness affected (relative paths from harness root)
- **symptom** — what the operator hits, in 1–3 sentences
- **proposed fix** — how to address upstream, in 1–3 sentences

## Optional inputs

- **references** — commits / docs / session logs that already discuss this
- **patched (project-local)** — what workaround was applied locally, if any
- **section** — which top-level section of `harness-backlog.md` to append to. Default: most recent project-kickoff or phase section.

## Steps

1. **Determine the entry prefix at runtime (v3.0.44 — instance-namespaced numbering, backlog v3.0-128).** In an instantiated project, read `project_slug` from `project.yaml`: the prefix is the slug itself (entries read `<project_slug>-N`, e.g. `example-project-4`). In the harness template repo itself (no instantiated `project.yaml`), the prefix stays `v<VERSION>` from the root `VERSION` file — upstream canon numbering is unchanged. Never guess the prefix or copy it from an older entry. **Why the slug:** three instances in one week minted local numbers colliding with upstream's — a slug prefix makes collision impossible by construction. Existing local entries under old `v<version>-N` numbers keep their numbers forever (stable-numbering rule); only NEW entries use the slug.
2. **Read `harness-backlog.md`** at the project root to find the highest existing N **for the current prefix**. The new entry's number is N+1; if no entry carries the current prefix yet, start at 1.
3. **Compose the entry** using the file's canonical format:
   ```markdown
   ### <prefix>-<N> — <Title>
   - **Surfaced:** <date and context>
   - **Where:** <file paths>
   - **Severity:** <severity>
   - **Symptom:** <one or two sentences>
   - **Patched (project-local):** <workaround applied, if any; omit field if none>
   - **Proposed fix:** <how to address upstream>
   - **References:** <links to commits / docs / session logs>
   ```
4. **Append to the appropriate section** of `harness-backlog.md`. Default: append after the last existing entry in the most recent project section. If creating a new section is warranted (e.g., the project entered a new phase), confirm with the operator before adding a section header.
5. **File upstream (v3.0.44, backlog v3.0-128) — only when `backlog_upstream` is set in `project.yaml`.** After the local append lands, run:
   `gh issue create --repo <backlog_upstream> --title "[<prefix>] <title>" --body-file <tempfile> --label fleet-backlog`
   where the tempfile is the origin line (`Filed from: <git remote get-url origin>`) followed by
   the entry exactly as it stands at filing time — i.e. before the issue URL is added back to
   References, which necessarily happens after the issue exists. Local and upstream then differ
   by exactly that one URL line, by design.
   and record the returned issue URL in the entry's **References** field. (Completing the
   just-written entry's own fields within the same invocation is part of composing that
   entry — the append-only rule forbids touching OTHER entries, not finishing this one.)
   Rules for this step:
   - The LOCAL append is the source of truth and always happens first — upstream is a copy, never the only home.
   - VISIBILITY CHECK IS MECHANICAL, not assumed: before filing, run
     `gh repo view <backlog_upstream> --json visibility` and proceed ONLY on `PRIVATE`.
     Anything else — `PUBLIC`, `INTERNAL`, an error, an ambiguous answer — means no
     filing: mark the entry `- **Upstream:** filing refused (<reason> <date>)` and tell
     the operator. Backlog entries may carry venture content; fail closed.
   - ANY failure of the `gh` filing itself — no gh, no auth, offline, permission denied,
     issues disabled, rate-limited, repo not found, anything — degrades the same way:
     append `- **Upstream:** filing pending (<reason> <date>)` to the local entry and
     move on. Failure text is never pasted into chat verbatim if it echoes the entry
     body; summarize the reason instead. Any later session that sees a pending marker
     may retry and replace the marker with the URL.
   - The issue body's FIRST line states the origin: `Filed from: <git remote get-url origin>` —
     so two instances that ever shared a slug remain distinguishable upstream (fleet slug
     uniqueness is the maintainer's registry duty, but the origin line makes a collision
     inconvenient rather than ambiguous).
   - Delete any temp file used for `--body-file` after the call.
   - If the `fleet-backlog` label doesn't exist upstream, file without the label rather than failing.
   - No secrets in the entry body, same as any commit — the entry travels to a remote.
   - One invocation, one filing: concurrency within an instance is governed by the
     single-writer session discipline, same as every other repo write.
   When `backlog_upstream` is absent, skip this step entirely (local-only is the designed fallback, not an error).
6. **Echo to the operator:** the entry number, title, a one-line summary, and — when step 5 ran — the upstream issue URL (or the pending marker). Confirm that the append landed.
7. **Do NOT modify other entries.** Each invocation appends one entry. Editing existing entries is out of scope (see Anti-patterns).

## Rules

- **Append-only.** Never rewrite, merge, or delete existing entries during a `/log-backlog` run.
- **One entry per invocation.** If the operator has multiple issues, run the skill once per issue. Batching corrupts the audit trail.
- **Don't auto-detect.** This skill logs an issue someone is explicitly raising. Suspicions in Claude's context that haven't been operator-endorsed don't qualify.
- **Stable numbering.** Once a number is assigned (old `v<version>-N` or new `<slug>-N` alike), it's permanent. If a later entry supersedes an earlier one, add a `Supersedes:` field naming it on the new entry; leave the original intact. Never renumber old entries to the slug scheme.
- **Upstream is additive.** Step 5 never replaces the local file; a project with no `backlog_upstream` key, no `gh`, or no network is fully functional local-only.

## Anti-patterns

- **Logging the same issue twice.** Before writing, scan existing entries by title or symptom. If a near-duplicate exists, surface it to the operator and ask whether to extend the existing entry's References field (manual edit; not via this skill) or skip.
- **Logging project-content issues.** A bug in this project's own deliverables or content is project-content; a bug in the harness's `/preflight` skill template is harness-template. The test: would this issue persist for ANY project instantiated from this harness, or is it specific to this one?
- **Editing fields after writing.** If an entry needs revision (typo, new evidence, severity change), make the edit manually — `/log-backlog` doesn't update existing entries. Or supersede with a new entry as above.
- **Skipping the read steps.** Always read the prefix source (`project.yaml` in an instance; the root `VERSION` file in the template repo, where no instantiated `project.yaml` exists) and `harness-backlog.md` (for the next N under that prefix) first. Don't guess either from memory — a stale prefix re-creates exactly the frozen-version drift this discipline was redesigned to kill.

## Output convention

After successful append, output:

```
Logged <prefix>-<N> — <title>. Severity: <severity>. Where: <file paths>.
Upstream: <issue URL | "filing pending" | "not configured">.

Next step: <if the operator should commit or amend, name it; otherwise omit>.
```

Keep the output tight. The point of `/log-backlog` is to make the ritual frictionless.
